23. AUTHENTICATION AND AUTHORISATION
==================================================

Implement a secure, maintainable authentication approach suitable for the selected deployment.

Do not assume that frontend role selectors provide security.

Requirements:
- Secure sign-in.
- Account verification where required.
- Password recovery if password authentication is used.
- Appropriate session management.
- Session expiry.
- Secure sign-out.
- Server-side authentication checks.
- Server-side role checks.
- Clinic-level authorisation.
- Organisation-level authorisation.
- Protection against privilege escalation.
- Appropriate rate limiting.
- Secure credential storage through the chosen authentication system.
- No hardcoded production credentials.
- No secret keys exposed in frontend bundles.

Use the least-privilege principle.

A patient must not be able to access another patient's records.

A practitioner must not automatically access every patient.

A clinic administrator must be restricted to their authorised clinic and administrative duties.

An organisation administrator must only receive the access required for their approved responsibilities.

Every sensitive API operation must verify the acting user's authority.

Demonstration role switching must never be mistaken for production authentication.

